<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-16" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Applications of Partial Derivatives</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this section, we turn our attention so applications of partial derivatives.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <objectives>
    <title>Applications of Partial Derivatives</title>
     <introduction>

    <p>
    In this section we will look at:
    </p>

  </introduction>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Calculating a tangent plane at a point.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Linear approximation in higher dimensions.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Directional derivatives and the gradient vector.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The second derivative test in three dimensions.
        </p>
      </li>
          </ul>
  </objectives>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-tangent-plane">
    <title>Tangent Planes</title>
    <p>
      Similar to a tangent line in two-dimensions, we can form a tangent plane in three-dimensions (or a hyperplane in higher dimensions). The equation of a tangent
          plane at the point <m>(x,y)=(a,b)</m>is 
          <md>
            L(x,y) = f(a,b) + f_x(a,b)(x-a) + f_y(a,b)(y-b)
          </md>
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Find the plane tangent to <m>f(x,y) = x^2 y+ xy^3</m> at the point (2, 1). Start by calculating the partial derivatives
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x = \, \amp 2xy + y^3</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y = \, \amp x^2 + 3xy^2</mrow>
        </md>
        Plug the point into the original function and partial derivatives.
        <md>
          <mrow>f(2,1) = \, \amp 4 + 2 = 6</mrow>
          <mrow>f_x(2,1) = \, \amp 4 + 1 = 5</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y(2,1) = \, \amp 4 + 6 = 10</mrow>
        </md>
        Plugging it all into the tangent plane formula and get
        <md>
          L(x,y) = 6 + 5(x-2) + 10(y-1)
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Here is a video with another examples.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Tangent Planes (6 minutes)</caption>
      <video youtube="wqtr4Udz8AY"/>
    </figure>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-linear-approximation">
    <title>Linear Approximation</title>
    <p>
      The tangent plane also defines a linear approximation (or first-degree Taylor approximation) of <m>f(x,y)</m> at a given point. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Estimate <m>(2.01)^3(1.02)^2</m> using a linear approximation. We can define a function <m>f(x,y)=x^3 y^2</m>. <m>f(2,3) = 2^3 1^2 = 8</m> is easily calculated, so
        we will use the point (2,3). The linear approximation, calculated in the same steps as the last example, is:
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x = \, \amp 3x^2 y^2</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y = \, \amp 2x^3 y</mrow>
          <mrow>f(2,1) = \, \amp 8</mrow>
          <mrow>f_x(2,1) = \, \amp 12</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y(2,1) = \,\amp 16</mrow>
          <mrow>L(x,y) = \, \amp 8 + 12(x-2) + 16(y-1)</mrow>
        </md>
        Plugging (2.01, 1.02) into the linear approximation gives
        <md>
          (2.01)^3(1.02)^2 \approx L(2.01, 1.02) = 8 + 12(2.01-2)+16(1.02-1) = 8.44
        </md>
        <m>(2.01)^3(1.02)^2 = 8.44867328</m>, so our approximation matches the first two decimal places.
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Let <m>B=W/H^2</m> defines a person's body mass index (BMI), where <m>W</m> is a person's weight in kilograms and <m>H</m> is a person's height in meters. Find
        the approximate BMI for a person weighs 89.7 kilograms and is 1.98 kilometers (198 centimeters) tall. The point <m>(W,H) = (90,2)</m> gives a BMI of <m>B = 22.5</m>.
        Calculating the derivatives and writing out the linear approximation gives:
        <md>
          <mrow>B_W=\, \amp \frac{1}{H^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>B_H=\, \amp -\frac{2W}{H^3}</mrow>
          <mrow>B(90,2)=\, \amp 22.5</mrow>
          <mrow>B_W(90,2)=\, \amp 0.25</mrow>
          <mrow>B_H(90,2)=\, \amp -22.5</mrow>
          <mrow>L(H,W)=\, \amp 22.5 + 0.25(W-90)-22.5(H-2)</mrow>
        </md>
        So <m>B(89.7,1.98) \approx L(89.7,1.98) = 22.5 + 0.25(89.7-90)-22.5(1.98-2) = 22.9425</m>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Here is a video with another examples.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Linear Approximation (10 minutes)</caption>
      <video youtube="hWyCDqjb-6A"/>
    </figure> 
    <paragraphs>
    <title>Reading Check-in</title>
    <p>
      Check our WAMAP course page for a few questions that go along with today's reading on tangent planes and linear approximation.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
  </subsection>
 
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-gradient">
    <title>The Directional Derivative and Gradient Vector</title>
    <p>
      A directional derivative, is a partial derivative that points along the vector <m>\mathbf{u}</m>. Note that <m>\mathbf{u}</m> is a vector. Recall that a vector
      has a magnitude (which equals 1 for the directional derivative) and direction and that to write a vector in component form given the vector's direction and magnitude,
      you get <m>\langle \cos(\theta), \cos(\theta)\rangle</m>. We will do something similar for the directional derivative where the partial derivatives play the role of
      the magnitude. The directional derivative along the unit vector <m>\mathbf{u}</m> with direction <m>\theta</m> is:
      <md>
        D_{\mathbf{v}}f(x,y) = f_x(x,y)\cos(\theta) + f_y(x,y)\sin(\theta)
      </md>
      </p>
      <example>
        <p>
          Find the directional derivative of <m>f(x,y) = x^2+2y^2</m> in the direction <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{4}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Calculate the partial derivatives
          <md>
            <mrow>f_x =\, \amp 2x</mrow>    
            <mrow>f_y =\, \amp 4y</mrow> 
          </md>
                    Then the directional derivative is:
          <m>D_{\mathbf{v}}f = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}2x+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}4y = \sqrt{2}x+2\sqrt{2}y</m>
        </p>
      </example>
      <example>
        <p>
          Find the directional derivative of <m>f(x,y) = e^x\sin(y)</m> along the vector <m>\langle \frac{1}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\rangle</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Calculate the partial derivatives
          <md>
            <mrow>f_x =\, \amp e^x\sin(y)</mrow>    
            <mrow>f_y =\, \amp e^x\cos(y)</mrow> 
          </md>
          Next find the direction of the vector
          <md>
            \theta = \arctan\left(\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}\right) = \frac{\pi}{3}
          </md>
          Then the directional derivative is:
          <md>D_{\mathbf{v}}f = \frac{1}{2}e^x\sin(y)+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}e^x\cos(y)</md>
        </p>
      </example>
      <p>
        Another way to define the directional derivative is using the gradient, The gradient vector is defined as <m>\nabla f(x,y) = \langle f_x,f_y \rangle</m>. 
        <m>\nabla f</m>  is read as "del f". Del and the gradient will be used often in Calculus IV. The directional derivative may also be defined
        using the gradient as <m>D_\mathbf{u}f(x,y) = \nabla f(x,y)\cdot\mathbf{u}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The gradient may also be used to find a plane tangent to a surface. Given a surface defined by an <em>equation</em> <m>F(x,y,z)=k</m> where <m>k</m> is a constant,
        then the tangent plane at point <m>p = (a,b,c)</m> is <m>\nabla F \cdot \langle x-a, y-b, z-c \rangle = 0</m>. For a function <m>z=f(x,y)</m> we can rewrite this
        as <m>F(x,y,z) = f(x,y)-z = 0</m>. The dot product here produces the same formula for the tangent plane given earlier.
      </p>
      <p>
      Here is a video with more examples.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Directional Derivative and Gradient (28 minutes)</caption>
      <video youtube="CnVes9TdnPo"/>
    </figure>
    <paragraphs>
    <title>Reading Check-in</title>
    <p>
      Check our WAMAP course page for a few questions that go along with today's reading on the directional derivative.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
        </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-3d-optimization">
    <title>The Second Derivative Test</title>
    <p>
      Finally, one of the most important applications is optimization; finding a maximum and minimum. In Calculus I (Math 151) we saw three ways to optimize a function
      using derivatives: the Extreme Value Theorem, the First Derivative Test and the Second Derivative Test. There is a multi-variable analog to the Extreme Value Theorem,
      called Lagrange multipliers that we will look at it in the next section. Recall that the First Derivative Test looks at what happens to the sign (positive or negative)
      of a derivative around a critical value as you move along number line from left to right. Since we can move from left to right (along the <m>x</m>-axis) along different
      <m>y-</m>lines in three dimensions, this would produce an infinite amount of results, so the First Derivative does not work in higher dimensions.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Second Derivative Test, which use concavity to find local maximum and minimum values, does work in three dimensions. Similar to the single-variable version of 
      the Second Derivative Test, we first find critical points and then plug them into the second derivative to test their concavity.
      Critical points in three dimensions are defined as points where:
      <md>
        <mrow>f_x(a,b) = 0 \,\,\,\text{or}\,\,\,\amp f_x(a,b) \,\,\,\text{does not exist, and}</mrow>
        <mrow>f_y(a,b) = 0 \,\,\,\text{or}\,\,\,\amp f_y(a,b) \,\,\,\text{does not exist.} </mrow>
      </md>
      At a critical value, if the three-dimensional surface, is concave up in all directions, then you are at a local minimum and if the surface is concave down in all 
      directions, then you are at a local maximum. If you are concave up in some directions but concave down in other directions, then you are at a saddle point. Since
      there are both mixed, second partial derivatives and second partial derivatives of a single variable, we also define the discriminant for the second derivatives 
      to tell us the concavity at a point as:
      <md>D(a,b) = f_{xx}(a,b)\cdot f_{yy}(a,b) - f_{xy}^2(a,b)</md>
      Then the Second Derivative Test is:
    </p>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-2D3D-test">
      <title>Second Derivative Test</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>P=(a,b)</m> be a critical point of <m>f(x,y)</m> and <m>D</m> be the discriminant of <m>f(x,y)</m>, then if:
        <md>
          <mrow>D\gt 0 \,\,\amp\text{and}\,\, f_{xx}(a,b)\gt 0, \,\,\text{then}\,\, f(a,b) \,\,\text{is a local minimum.} </mrow>
          <mrow>D\gt 0 \,\,\amp\text{and}\,\, f_{xx}(a,b)\lt 0, \,\,\text{then}\,\, f(a,b) \,\,\text{is a local maximum.} </mrow>
          <mrow>D\lt 0,  \,\,\amp\text{then}\,\, f(a,b) \,\,\text{is a saddle point.} </mrow>
          <mrow>D = 0, \,\,\amp\text{then the test is inconclusive.} </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
    </assemblage>
    <example>
      <p>
        Find and classify the critical points for the function <m>f(x,y)=x^3+y^3-12xy</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Start by taking te partial derivatives:
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x =\, \amp 3x^2-12y</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y =\, \amp 3y^2-12x</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{xx} =\, \amp 6x</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{yy} =\, \amp 6y</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{xy} =\, \amp -12y</mrow>
        </md>
        Next find the critical points. (This is usually the most work for these problems.)
        
          <md> f_x = 0\, =3x^2-1y \quad\to\quad y = \frac{1}{4}x^2 </md>
          <md> f_y = 0\, =3x^2-1y \,\,\,\text{plug the first equation into this second equation to get}</md>
          <md> 3(x^2/4)^2 - 12x = \frac{3}{16}(x^3-64) \,\,\to\,\, x = 0, 4</md>
          <md>\text{plugging these x-values into the first equation to get the critical points}</md>
          <md> (0,0)\,\,\text{and}\,\,(4,4)</md>
        
        Plug these into the discriminant and second derivative to get 
        <md>
          <mrow>D(0,0) = -144 \lt 0\quad \amp \text{saddle point at (0,0)} </mrow>
          <mrow>D(4,4) = 432 \gt 0\,\,\text{and}\,\, f_{xx} = 24 \gt 0\quad \amp \text{local minimum at (4,4)} </mrow>
        </md>
        Try plotting this surface to verify the solutions.
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Find and classify the critical points for the function <m>f(x,y)=4x^2+9y^2+8x-36y+24</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Start by taking te partial derivatives:
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x =\, \amp 8x+8</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y =\, \amp 18y-36</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{xx} =\, \amp 8</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{yy} =\, \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{xy} =\, \amp 18</mrow>
        </md>
        Next find the critical points.
        
          <md> 8x+8=0 \quad \to \quad x = -1 </md>
          <md> 18y-36=0 \quad \to \quad y=2 </md>
        
        The only critical point is (-1,2) Plug this into the discriminant and second derivative to get 
        <md>
        D(-1,2) = 144 \gt 0 \,\,\text{and}\,\, f_{xx} = 8 \gt 0
        </md>
        So (-1, 2) is a local minimum.
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
       A shipping company handles rectangular boxes provided the sum of the length, width, and height of the box does not exceed 96 inches.
       Find the dimensions of the box that meets this condition and has the largest volume.
      </p>
      <p>
        Define two equations based on the given information.
        <md>
          l+w+h = 96 \quad \text{and} \quad V=lwh
        </md>
        Solve the first equation for one of the variables and plug the result into the second equation, eliminating one of the variables. I will eliminate <m>h</m>.
        <md>
          h= 96-l-w \quad \to \quad V=lw(96-l-w) = 96lw - l^2w-lw^2
        </md>
        This is the equation we will maximize.
        <md>
          <mrow>V_l =\, \amp 96w - 2lw - w^2</mrow>
          <mrow>V_w =\, \amp 96l - l^2 - 2lw</mrow>
          <mrow>V_{ll} = \, \amp -2w </mrow>
          <mrow>V_{ww} = \, \amp -2l </mrow>
          <mrow>V_{lw} = \, \amp 96 - 2l -2w </mrow>
        </md>
        Find the critical values
        <md>
          <mrow>96w-2lw-w^2 = 0 \quad\amp \to w(96-2l-w)=0</mrow>
          <mrow>96l-l^2-2lw = 0 \quad\amp \to l(96-2w-l)=0</mrow>
        </md>
        Which gives one critical point at (0,0). Solve <m>96-2l-w = 0 </m> for <m>w</m> and plug it into the second for the second equation to get
        <md>
          96 - 2(96-2l)-l = 0 \quad \to \quad -96 +3l = 0 \quad \to \quad l = 32
        </md>
        Which when substituted back in the first equation gives <m>w=32</m> and we get a second critical point at (32,32). The critical point at (0, 0) gives a volume of
        zero, which in the context of the problem is the minimum possible volume. <m>D(32,32) = -2(32)(-2)(32)-(-32)^2 = 3072 \gt 0</m> and <m>V_{ll}(32,32) = -64 \lt 0</m>
        and (32, 32) is at a local maximum. The maximum volume occurs when <m>l=w=h=32</m> for a maximum volume of <m>V=32^3 = 32,768</m> cubic inches.
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Here is a video with a few more examples:
    </p>
    <figure>
    <caption>Second Derivative Test</caption>
    <video youtube="UID893EosM8"/>
  </figure>
